"""Content E: Elves & Dwarfs chapter.
Tuple formats:
  ("lore", kicker, title, [paras], prompt)
  ("journal", kicker, prompt, lines)
  ("tracker", kicker, prompt, [row labels])
Every lore page's last para is its Where-this-comes-from line.
Voice: plain, 2nd-person. Plain voice; sources named, modern and rebuilt practices said so.
Cut: Elfshots (duplicates Liminal Places sickness para), Svartálfaheimr
standalone (folded into Bright/dark page), extra journals + edge/forge
tracker held in cuttings.py. CH_ED is the 8 lore pages that ship.
"""

CH_ELVES = [
("lore", "Elves", "Bright elves and dark elves",
 ["In the Poetic Edda, elves are shy neighbors. You mostly glimpse them at the edge of lamplight. The Grímnismál names Álfheimr as their home. Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda divides them further. He calls one group light-elves, bright as the sun to look at. He calls the other group dark-elves, black as pitch, who live down in the earth. The poems often pair elves with the gods in the phrase “Æsir and elves,” and the Lokasenna seats them at Ægir’s feast. They are witnesses from the start.",
   "The line between the two groups is loose. The Poetic Edda never draws it cleanly. The light-elves and dark-elves appear in Snorri’s Prose Edda, but his split is late and tidy. Some scholars think he may be sorting dwarfs under another name, calling them the smiths of Svartálfaheimr, the dark-elf world. The Poetic Edda names elves often but describes them rarely. They are beautiful and dangerous outsiders, tied to mounds and fields. They are neither angels nor demons. Treat the boundary as blurred, and do not force it.",
   "Think of the elves as unseen neighbors who watch from the edge. You can honor them by stepping softly, keeping paths clear, and leaving one quiet place alone. Practice this in one corner of your week, not in all of it.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Álfheimr is in Grímnismál, and the light and dark split is in Snorri’s Prose Edda, which is late and tidy."],
 "Name one spot near your home that feels quietly watched over. Name how you will keep it. Give it two dated lines."),
("lore", "Elves", "Freyr: lord of Álfheimr",
 ["One gift ties the elves to the harvest. The Grímnismál says Álfheimr was given to Freyr as a tooth-gift in ancient days, a gift marking a first tooth, as the poem tells it. The god was young and of the Vanir. The Skírnismál sends Freyr’s servant to court Gerðr for him across worlds, and the servant offers her gift after gift. No verse in the Poetic Edda explains what lordship meant in practice.",
   "Lordship here means something like neighborliness, not rule. The Poetic Edda gives Freyr a title but lists no duties. The tooth-gift verse is in Grímnismál, and it is short and plain. The gift binds the elves to him the way two farms share a fence. Any rite that treats Freyr as king of the elves, with set prayers, is modern. This book sets that aside. Read the verse itself before you build any practice on it.",
   "Freyr is linked to fair weather and good harvests on tame ground. Elves are linked to beauty and danger at the wild edge. You can see that border simply: put a pot by your door with tended soil inside, and leave wild seed heads beyond it. Keeping tame and wild in balance is the everyday task. Mending the fence keeps the harvest safe.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The tooth-gift is in Grímnismál. This book adds no elf-king rites."],
 "Which place in your life joins tame to wild? Name it, name how you tend it, and log it with the date in two lines."),
("lore", "Elves", "Álfablót (Alfablot): the closed door",
 ["In autumn, a traveler in Norway found the farms shut against him. That traveler was Sigvatr Þórðarson (Thordarson), a skald, meaning a court poet, of King Óláfr (Olaf). In his poem Austrfararvísur (Austrfararvisur), he tells how he went from farm to farm seeking a bed and was turned away. Each farm, he says, was holding an álfablót (alfablot), an elf sacrifice, which was holy and closed to strangers. Blood was offered and the doors were barred. No guest was taken in. That closed door is nearly all outsiders saw of the rite.",
   "This is the honest version. Sigvatr’s poem is a witness written at about the same time. It dates from the early eleventh century. It describes being shut out, not a set ritual (liturgy). No prayer text, altar plan, or list of offerings survives in the Poetic Edda, Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda, or Egils saga. Later farm custom, by contrast, leaves food and drink at mounds and keeps certain hills alone. That is folk custom, meaning ordinary practice passed down, and it is not the same rite. The sources support only a few plain points: autumn, closed doors, and family only.",
   "Picture one farm of the kind Sigvatr describes. It barred its door early and kept the evening shut on purpose. This is one skald’s account of farms, not a rule for every farm. You can keep the same spirit at home. Pick one evening, close the door to noise, eat quietly, and speak one thank-you aloud.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The álfablót is in Sigvatr’s Austrfararvísur, a poem from the time. No account of the rite itself survives."],
 "When will you bar your door early for one quiet meal with thanks? Name the date, the meal, and the people you will thank. Give it three dated lines."),
("lore", "Elves", "Elves in the ground",
 ["Elves are still present in place names long after the Poetic Edda falls quiet. Farms and fields across Norway and Sweden carry elf-names, such as Álfheimr districts and Alf-farmsteads. Some stones and mounds were left alone by plows, while the plows cut straight through everything else. Icelandic sagas describe mounds honored with toasts poured at the door-stone and paths swept clean. The dead inside were called mound-dwellers (the people who lie in the hills), and they were not always clearly separate from elves.",
   "This is about the land, not about formal teachings. Place names last longer than the books that record them. That elf-names mark the land is shown on stones and in farm-names recorded in Landnámabók. Folk custom still shows farmers leaving certain mounds and hills alone, within recent memory. Iceland’s huldufolk, the hidden people, kept the same courtesy the longest. Your street may keep a version of this too, perhaps the corner no one builds on, or the tree no one cuts.",
   "The plow went straight everywhere else but turned aside for one stone. Toasts were poured at the door-stone, and paths were swept for the mound-dwellers. The names stayed on the map. You can do the same on your block: leave one tree, one patch of ground, or one corner alone. The courtesy is simple. Leave it be, and do not take what is not yours.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Elf place-names and mound-honor are in old records and folk custom."],
 "Walk your block once this week and look for places people leave alone. Mark two spots. Give each one dated line."),
("lore", "Elves", "Honoring without summoning",
 ["Elves were never worshipped the way the gods were. That limit is part of the practice. No temple to elves has been found by archaeologists. No priest is named for them. The one sign of honor on record is the closed autumn door. Offerings at mounds and hills in Icelandic sagas were small. They were poured or laid down and left alone, with no attempt to call anything up. The Lokasenna seats the elves among the gods at Ægir’s feast. They are guests and witnesses, not servants.",
   "The old practice was courtesy without command. People cleaned up edges and lowered noise near nesting ground. They asked leave before taking, and they gave thanks after. No answer or sign was demanded. The mound toasts are a pattern in Icelandic sagas. The quiet-edge practice is rebuilt from that pattern, and this book says so plainly. Nothing was summoned or bound. Nothing was bought. This is good manners, not control.",
   "Nothing was summoned and nothing was bound. Keep one edge this way: choose one patch of ground or one shared space. Ask before you take from it, give thanks after, and leave it quieter than you found it. That is all this practice asks.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Mound offerings are in Icelandic sagas. This book offers no summoning rites."],
 "Which edge will you keep this month, using only good manners? Name place, act, and date in three lines."),
]

CH_DWARFS = [
("lore", "Dwarfs", "Born from earth and sea",
 ["The Völuspá gives dwarfs the longest list of names in the Poetic Edda, but almost no description. After the gods shape the world, the dwarfs come to life in the earth, like maggots that grow in flesh, according to Snorri’s Prose Edda. In the Völuspá they are shaped from Ymir’s bones and from the blood of the sea-giant Brimir. The gods give them mind and shape. Then comes a long list of names: Northri and Southri, Austri and Vestri, Gandalf and Thorin. Many of the names point to stone, staff, or advice.",
   "Think of the dwarfs first as powers of the earth, and only second as people. The dwarf list stands in the Völuspá. Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda retells the making plainly. It is late, but faithful to the story. The Poetic Edda places them in rock and earth, and they avoid daylight and work metal. Later books make them short, comic miners with axes. That picture is modern. The Poetic Edda shows skill, advice, and cost. It shows no comedy.",
   "The dwarfs came to life in the earth from Ymir’s bones and Brimir’s blood, and the gods gave them mind and shape. Four of their names match the four directions: Northri, Southri, Austri, and Vestri. Stand in your home and face each direction in turn. Name the wall or corner that faces each one. Knowing a place well helps you move through it with care.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The dwarf origins and name lists are in the Völuspá and Snorri’s Prose Edda, which is late."],
 "Which three dwarf-names will you learn first? Name them, name their corners, and give three dated lines."),
("lore", "Dwarfs", "Treasures from the forge",
 ["The gods could not have their finest tools without dwarfs to make them, and every gift had a cost. Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda, in its Skáldskaparmál (Skaldskaparmal), tells it plainly. Loki cut the hair of Sif, Þórr’s wife, for sport, and then he had to pay for it. He got new hair of gold from the Ívaldi (Ivaldi) sons, plus Skíðblaðnir, a ship that folds up, and Gungnir, a spear that never misses. Then Loki bet his head against the dwarfs Brokkr and Sindri. They made Draupnir, a gold ring that drips more gold, Gullinbursti, a golden boar, and Mjölnir, a hammer with a short handle.",
   "The cost matters more than the shine. Every treasure costs something. Loki’s head was on the line, and only his wordplay saved his neck. The hammer’s handle is short because Loki, as a fly, stung the smith’s brow while he worked. The treasure tale is in the Skáldskaparmál. It is late, but full of detail. No dwarf-worship follows from it, and this book builds none. The lesson is simpler. Good work costs effort. Careless tricks with other people’s goods bring trouble.",
   "Sif’s gold hair shows the pattern: a careless wrong is repaired by fine work, and the repair is paid for. Use that lesson in your own life. Fine work deserves to be honored, not just owned.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Ívaldi and Brokkr treasures are in Skáldskaparmál, a late prose work."],
 "Which fine-made thing of yours needs mending? Name it, name its maker, and log the price and date in four lines."),
("lore", "Dwarfs", "Völundr (Volund) the smith",
 ["A lame smith on an island takes revenge, and the story still chills. The Völundarkviða (Volundarkvida) tells it. Völundr is the greatest of the elven-smiths. King Níðuðr (Nidud) hamstrung him, cutting the tendons in his legs so he could not walk well, and made him forge on the king’s island. Völundr killed the king’s sons in revenge for what was done to him. He made cups from their skulls. Then he flew away on wings of his own making, and the king was left grieving. The poem holds craft, captivity, revenge, and flight.",
   "Völundr is a warning, not a patron, meaning a protecting figure you honor. The poem does not make him gentle. The tale is in the Poetic Edda, and it reads as stark and early. Honoring Völundr with a hammer and forge is a modern craft practice, honest only when called so. The lesson is plain: skilled people who serve a household should not be held against their will. Skill does not give anyone the right to force another person. Forced work is cruel.",
   "Völundr was forced to work on the king’s island and could not walk well. He took revenge, flew away, and left the king grieving. Pay the people who make things for you fairly, and thank them by name. Work freely given and fairly paid is the everyday opposite of Völundr’s chains.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> Völundr’s story is in the Völundarkviða in the Poetic Edda. Smith-patron rites are modern."],
 "Whose skilled work do you rely on without thanks? Name that person, what you owe, and one way you will thank them. Give three dated lines."),
("lore", "Dwarfs", "Alvíss (Alvis): all-wise till dawn",
 ["One dwarf stayed talking until dawn, and that was his undoing. The Alvíssmál tells it plainly. Alvíss the dwarf comes to claim Þórr’s daughter. He asks for her as his bride-price, a payment to the bride’s family. Þórr does not refuse him outright. He answers the dwarf with riddles about what each world calls earth, sky, and ale. He keeps the dwarf talking until sunrise. In a later retelling, daylight turns dwarfs to stone, and that is how Alvíss ends, like other dwarfs. Þórr wins with talk and delay until dawn.",
   "Þórr’s method is plain common sense. The poem gives a method, not magic. The dawn trap for Alvíss is told in the Alvíssmál, a full dialogue with a dwarf. That sunlight turns dwarfs to stone is a later telling. I include it lightly and say so. The lesson still applies. Answer a hard bargain with questions, not force. Sleep on a deal, and it may look different in daylight.",
   "Alvíss talked at the wedding-door through riddles about earth, sky, and ale. Þórr answered with delay until sunrise. You can use the same tactic on a hard bargain: wait for daylight, and make the other side answer your questions in writing.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The Alvíssmál is a poem in the Poetic Edda. Daylight turning dwarfs to stone is a later telling."],
 "What pressure wants you to sign now? Name it, write its three questions, and sleep before signing. Give dated lines tonight."),
("lore", "Dwarfs", "Stone: iron and honest work",
 ["Dwarfs are pictured as makers, not as distant blessers. They make things, finish them, and hand them over with a fair price. Runestones carry the old letters, cut in a set pattern. Rings are weighed true, meaning their weight is honest. Blades hold their edge. Doors shut square. Smithies stood apart from the halls because of the fire. A marked tool outlived its maker by generations. The old north valued kept edges over fine words. Edges were kept, debts were paid, and names were cut plain.",
   "Here is the practice in plain terms. It is rebuilt: this book builds it from the pattern above, and it is simple enough to keep. Keep a toolbox in order and one edge sharp. Commission one repair fairly, and speak one maker’s name with thanks. The forge treasures are in Skáldskaparmál. The toolbox habit is a modern household craft built on that pattern. Doing the work comes before talking about it.",
   "A sharp knife comes from steady honing, not from talk about it. This week, spend one hour on a tool or a fix, and let your hands learn the job, one step at a time.",
   "<b>Where this comes from:</b> The forge treasures are in Skáldskaparmál. The toolbox practice was rebuilt for this book."],
 "Which tool, hinge, or hem is yours to fix this week? Name it, name the time you will give it, and log tool, work, and thanks in three dated lines."),
]

CH_ED = CH_ELVES + CH_DWARFS
